\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Odd equivariant coefficients}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let a group #{G} act linearly on a module
#{V=V_+\oplus V_-} over a unital ring, preserving each
summand. A linear endomorphism taking each summand into the
other and commuting with #{G} is specified by an arbitrary
pair
##{b_+\in\operatorname{Hom}_G(V_+,V_-),\qquad
 b_-\in\operatorname{Hom}_G(V_-,V_+).}
It is invertible exactly when both maps are isomorphisms.
In particular, for sectors given by the two sign eigenspaces of a
grading #{\Gamma}, any invertible Clifford direction commuting
with #{G} and anticommuting with #{\Gamma} forces the sectors
to be isomorphic. The zero coefficient exists for
every pair of sectors. In a complex Hermitian space with an
orthogonal decomposition the coefficient is self-adjoint
exactly when #{b_-=b_+^\dagger}.}

\proof{
\p{The direct sum expresses each odd endomorphism uniquely as
#{(v_+,v_-)\mapsto(b_-v_-,b_+v_+)}. Evaluate commutation
with #{G} separately on each summand to obtain the two
intertwining conditions. Injectivity of the whole map is
equivalent to injectivity of both restrictions, and its image
is #{\im b_-\oplus\im b_+}. Bijectivity is therefore equivalent
to bijectivity of both, giving the criterion of \ref{fdip-018C}
when an invertible coefficient exists. Anticommuting with
#{\Gamma} sends a sign eigenvector into the opposite sign
space. Taking both restrictions
zero gives the zero endomorphism. In the orthogonal Hermitian
sum, pairing a vector in each summand with the image of a vector
in the other gives exactly the adjoint identity.}
}
